/// \defgroup grp_w1_commandline Command-line switch handlers
/// \ingroup grp_engine_core
/// \brief The functions behind the game's command-line switches, such as
///        `-mod`, `-file`, `-win` and `-noshellmap`.
///
/// GameEngine::init registers `TheWritableGlobalData` and immediately calls
/// parseCommandLine with the process arguments. The dispatcher looks each
/// argument up in a table in the retail binary that pairs a switch name with
/// a handler function, and calls the handler it finds. Every function in this
/// group except parseFPUPreserve() is one such handler, and the switch it
/// serves comes from that retail table, not from the function's name. Most
/// handlers write a setting into the global data (`GlobalData`) that later
/// code reads; a few set a process-wide flag or a field of `TheVersion`
/// instead.
///
/// Every handler has the same shape, `int handler(char *args[], int num)`:
/// - `args[0]` is the switch itself and `args[1]`, when present, its value.
/// - `num` counts the arguments left on the command line, the switch
///   included. A handler that takes a value reads `args[1]` only when `num`
///   is greater than 1.
/// - The return value is how many arguments the dispatcher skips:
///   normally 1 for a switch alone and 2 for a switch and its value. The
///   exceptions on this page are: parseRandomSeed() returns 1 even after
///   reading a value; parseFile(), parseResumeGame() and parseFPUPreserve()
///   return 2 even when no value follows; parseMod() returns 2 even when it
///   rejects the path; and parseMod(), parseXRes(), parseYRes() and
///   parseFullVersion() return 1 when the object they write does not exist,
///   even if a value follows. Each handler's page gives its return values.
///   Of the handlers on this page, parseFullVersion() and parseRandomSeed()
///   return a different count from their Zero Hour counterparts; their pages
///   say so.
///
/// What follows about BFME 2's parseCommandLine was read from its retail
/// bytes and is stated at contract level:
/// - A helper walks the arguments from the second one on. It compares the
///   whole argument with each switch name in the table, ignoring case,
///   calls the matching handler and advances by its return value, and skips
///   an argument that matches nothing. Before comparing, it turns a first
///   byte of 0x96 (the Windows-1252 en dash) into a hyphen, as WinMain has
///   already done for every argument. Because WinMain converts the command
///   line to UTF-8 first, a real en dash arrives as a different byte
///   sequence, does not match and is left alone (see \ref page_startup).
/// - It then loads the mod that `-mod` recorded: a mod BIG file, or every
///   `.BIG` file in a mod folder, goes into the BIG archive back end with
///   the overwrite argument set. A mod folder also becomes the first folder
///   searched for loose files. parseMod() has the details.
/// - Last, it computes a checksum over the process-wide mask of
///   command-line options, together with the bytes that follow it, and
///   stores it in the global data. Online and LAN joins use it (see
///   parseRif()).
///
/// The donors' dispatchers, Zero Hour's and the BFME 1 retail one as
/// Open-BFME-1 rebuilt it, run the same loop (the 0x96 byte step is in
/// BFME 1's) and then load the mod archives with overwrite as well. Neither
/// has the checksum step.
///
/// | Switch | Handler | Effect |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | `-mod` path | parseMod() | Record a mod folder or BIG file, which the dispatcher loads over the game's archives after the switch loop; also sets the `-preferLocalFiles` flag |
/// | `-preferLocalFiles` | parsePreferLocalFiles() | Search loose files before BIG archives |
/// | `-file` path | parseFile() | Map or replay to open at startup |
/// | `-resumeGame` path | parseResumeGame() | As `-file`, plus a resume request flag |
/// | `-noshellmap` | parseNoShellMap() | No shell map (the map shown behind the menus) |
/// | `-noaudio` | parseNoAudio() | Turn every audio category off; also changes the command-line checksum that joins use |
/// | `-win` | parseWin() | Run in a window |
/// | `-xres` n, `-yres` n | parseXRes(), parseYRes() | Screen width and height |
/// | `-randomSeed` n | parseRandomSeed() | Fixed seed that overrides the seed games and replays start with |
/// | `-fullVersion` n | parseFullVersion() | Set `TheVersion`'s show-full-version flag (Zero Hour: full version in the options menu; BFME 2 reader not traced) |
/// | `-scriptDebug2`, `-scriptDebugLite` | parseScriptDebug2(), parseScriptDebugLite() | Load the script debugger window |
/// | `-Watchdog`, `-noWatchdog` | parseWatchdog(), parseNoWatchdog() | Start the watchdog thread, or not |
/// | `-rif` | parseRif() | A request flag whose meaning is not known; also changes the command-line checksum that joins use |
/// | none | parseFPUPreserve() | Not in the retail table; would keep the FPU mode when Direct3D starts |
///
/// BFME 2's retail table has sixteen entries, and this page covers all of
/// them. The first twelve are BFME 1's retail table in the same order:
/// `-noshellmap`, `-mod`, `-noaudio`, `-xres`, `-yres`, `-win`, both script
/// debugger switches, `-fullVersion`, `-preferLocalFiles` and both watchdog
/// switches. BFME 2 adds `-rif`, `-file`, `-resumeGame` and `-randomSeed`.
/// Zero Hour's release table differs more: it has `-fullscreen` and
/// `-scriptDebug`, for example, and keeps `-noaudio`, `-file` and its seed
/// switch for debug and internal builds. The other functions beside
/// parseNoAudio() in `CommandLineNoAudio.cpp`, most of which turn on CRC
/// options, are not in the table and are not covered here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The switch names and their handlers are fixed in code. Nothing is read
///   from data, so adding a switch or changing what one does is a code
///   change.
/// - `-mod` loads a mod's BIG file, or every `.BIG` file in a mod folder,
///   over the game's own archives without replacing the archives on disk.
///   A mod folder is also searched first for loose files. In Zero Hour the
///   overwrite setting makes a mod's files replace same-named game files;
///   see parseMod() for what is known in BFME 2.
/// - WinMain builds the argument list before the engine exists and keeps at
///   most 32 arguments, the program name included. Later arguments are
///   dropped.
/// - The handlers only record settings; none of them changes game logic
///   state itself. One setting does reach the game logic later:
///   `-randomSeed` replaces the seed that game starts and replay playback
///   pass to the random number generators (see parseRandomSeed()).
/// - `-noaudio` and `-rif` also set bits in the mask of command-line options
///   whose checksum the online custom-match join compares and a LAN join
///   request carries (see parseRif()). Players whose command lines differ in
///   these switches may therefore be refused with the CRC-mismatch message;
///   this is inferred from the code and has not been tested. `-randomSeed`
///   sets no bit in this mask, so a mismatch is not caught at join. It does
///   change the logic seed (see the bullet above and parseRandomSeed()), so
///   a player started with it would play from a different seed than the
///   others (not tested). Whether any other switch can make machines
///   disagree has not been established.
/// - GameEngine::init computes an INI checksum while it loads data. While
///   it registers its subsystems, the subsystem list holds the checksum
///   writer, and SubsystemInterface::loadIniFilesFromLegend loads the files
///   and directories that `SubsystemLegend.ini` lists for a subsystem
///   through that writer, apart from paths the legend excludes. It does so
///   whatever writer the registration call passes; that writer is used only
///   for fallback files when the legend loads nothing for the subsystem.
///   Which data is checksummed is therefore decided by the legend, which is
///   data, plus `Water.ini`, `Fire.ini` and `Environment.ini`, which
///   GameEngine::init loads through the writer directly, plus the
///   Create-a-Hero data's own checksum (CreateAHeroCRC::getCRC), which it
///   adds to the result. `SubsystemLegend.ini` and `CommandMap.ini` load
///   without the writer. A subsystem can replace the legend step: the award
///   system does, and its own load of `Data\INI\AwardSystem.ini` passes no
///   writer. The online join screen compares the result, a LAN join request
///   carries it, and replay code compares it with the value stored in a
///   replay's header (\ref page_startup_modding).
/// - Not all checksummed data loads after the mod archives. The global
///   data's legend files are checksummed, but GameEngine::init reads them
///   before parseCommandLine loads the `-mod` archives. Data loaded after
///   parseCommandLine, including `Water.ini`, `Fire.ini`, `Environment.ini`
///   and the legend files of the subsystems registered later, is expected
///   to come from a mod's copies where the mod supplies them, and so to feed
///   the checksum. This is an inference: how the INI loader finds files
///   through the archives has not been traced.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - WinMain reads the Unicode command line and converts it to UTF-8 before
///   splitting it, so the handlers see UTF-8 text. WinMain also scans for
///   `-win`, `-fullscreen`, `-xpos` and `-ypos` itself, to create the window,
///   separately from parseWin(). A new front end or launcher has to keep both
///   readers in step.
/// - Of the table's handlers, only parseMod() touches the disk: it queries it
///   with the wide-character C runtime. The path conversion behind `-file`
///   and `-resumeGame` reads the local clock for its label. The dispatcher's
///   own mod step then loads archives from disk. parseFPUPreserve(), which
///   no switch reaches, would change how the Direct3D device is created.
///
/// \see \ref sub_engine_core, \ref page_startup


/// \file CommandLineMod.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The `-mod` handler, parseMod().


/// \fn int parseMod(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-mod`: records a mod folder or a mod BIG file for the
///        file system to load.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-mod` with this function. The path is
/// stored in the global data, in one of two fields: one for a folder of
/// mod archives and one for a single archive file. The switch also sets the
/// same process-wide flag as `-preferLocalFiles` (parsePreferLocalFiles()), so
/// that loose files on disk are searched before the BIG archives.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The flag is set first and unconditionally, even when the switch has no
///   value or the global data does not exist yet.
/// - The value is converted to a wide (UTF-16) string. A path that contains
///   a colon or starts with a slash or backslash is used as it is. Any other
///   path is taken as relative to a folder that the global data supplies:
///   the same folder that UserPreferences::load reads its files from (Zero
///   Hour: the user-data folder).
/// - The path must exist, checked on disk through `TheFileSystem`, and
///   `_wstat` must succeed. If either fails the switch is ignored silently.
/// - A folder gets a trailing backslash if it has no trailing separator and
///   goes into the mod-folder field; anything else goes into the mod-file
///   field. Both fields are 8-bit strings, so the wide path is converted
///   back when it is stored.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch; `args[1]` is the path.
/// \param num  Number of arguments left, the switch included.
/// \return 1 when there is no value or no global data; otherwise 2, even
///         when the path was rejected.
///
/// \note BFME 1's handler is Zero Hour's version, plus setting the
///       local-files flag and checking existence through `TheFileSystem`
///       instead of `TheLocalFileSystem`; BFME 2 adds the wide path. Zero
///       Hour and BFME 1 also load the recorded mod into the archive file
///       system when parseCommandLine finishes.
///
/// \par Loading the mod in BFME 2
/// Read from the retail bytes of parseCommandLine. After the switch loop it
/// checks the two fields, the file first:
/// - A mod BIG file is handed to the BIG archive back end, which opens it
///   through the disk back end, checks for a `BIGF` or `BIG4` header and
///   adds the archive's entries with the overwrite argument set.
/// - A mod folder is copied into the folder prefix that
///   FileSystem::openFile and FileSystem::getFileInfo try first, the
///   `-preferLocalFiles` flag is set again, and the BIG archive back end
///   loads every file in the folder that matches `*.BIG`, again with
///   overwrite set.
///
/// Both steps run inside GameEngine::init after it has loaded
/// `SubsystemLegend.ini` and registered the global data, and before it loads
/// `Water.ini` and the INI data loaded after it. Registering a subsystem
/// reads any INI files that the legend lists for it
/// (SubsystemInterfaceList::initSubsystem), so whatever the legend loads for
/// the global data is read before the mod's archives are loaded.
///
/// In Zero Hour, overwrite means an archive's files replace entries with the
/// same path that are already loaded; BFME 2's insert step has not been
/// traced that far. Which back end reads BIG archives is subject to the
/// warning on parsePreferLocalFiles().
///
/// \par Other readers in BFME 2
/// W3DDisplay::init passes both fields to InitializeAssetManager as its
/// directory and archive arguments. InitializeAssetManager reads an
/// `asset.dat` from the mod BIG file (it checks for a `BIGF` or `BIG4`
/// header and looks for an entry with that name) and then from the mod
/// folder, before it reads the `asset.dat` in the current folder. The link
/// between these arguments and parseMod()'s two fields rests on their
/// position in the global data, not on the names the two source files use
/// for them.
///
/// \par Modding
/// A relative path is resolved against the folder described above, not
/// against the game's install folder; pass a full path to load a mod from
/// anywhere else. A misspelt path gives no error message. A mod BIG file,
/// or every `.BIG` file in a mod folder, is loaded with overwrite on top of
/// the game's archives, and a mod folder is also the first folder searched
/// for loose files (parsePreferLocalFiles()). The mod's own `asset.dat`, in
/// its BIG file or its folder, is read before the game's. For the INI
/// checksum that online joins compare and replays check, and what is known
/// about a mod's effect on it, see the group description.
///
/// \see parsePreferLocalFiles(), FileSystem::openFile, UserPreferences::load,
///      W3DDisplay::init, InitializeAssetManager


/// \file CommandLineLocalFiles.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The `-preferLocalFiles` handler, parsePreferLocalFiles(), and the flag it
///        sets.


/// \fn int parsePreferLocalFiles(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-preferLocalFiles`: searches loose files on disk before
///        the BIG archives.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-preferLocalFiles` with this function. It
/// sets a process-wide flag and nothing else. The flag's name in the source
/// is descriptive; its effect comes from its readers:
/// - FileSystem::openFile and FileSystem::getFileInfo, when the flag is set,
///   ask the back end that checks files on disk first. They try the name
///   under the folder recorded by `-mod` (only when `-mod` named a folder),
///   then under the current language's folder in `lang` (for example
///   `lang\English`), then the name itself, and then fall back to the BIG
///   archives. When the flag is clear they ask the BIG archives first, then
///   the disk, without the folder variants.
/// - The source calls the first prefix's buffer `TheLangDir`, which suggests
///   a language folder, but its only writer in the retail code is the
///   dispatcher's mod-folder step, which stores the `-mod` folder there. The
///   language name starts as `English`; GameEngine::init replaces it with
///   the result of GetRegistryLanguage before it creates the file system's
///   two back ends.
/// - parseMod() sets the same flag, the dispatcher's mod-folder step sets it
///   again (see parseMod()), and the file system's own setup code sets it in
///   some cases.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch (not read).
/// \param num  Number of arguments left (not read).
/// \return Always 1.
///
/// \par Modding
/// With the flag set, a loose file on disk takes the place of the archived
/// file with the same path, which is convenient while developing a mod.
/// Because `-mod` and the file system's setup can set the flag too, it may
/// already be on without this switch. The search order itself is fixed in
/// the two file system functions above.
///
/// \warning The roles given here (which back end checks loose files on disk
///          and which reads BIG archives) come from the back ends'
///          constructors and existence tests, not from the names of the two
///          back-end globals in the reconstructed source, which may not
///          match those roles. Go by the roles described here, not by the
///          spelling of the globals.
/// \see parseMod(), FileSystem::openFile, FileSystem::getFileInfo


/// \file CommandLineFile.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The `-file` and `-resumeGame` handlers, parseFile() and
///        parseResumeGame().


/// \fn int parseFile(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-file`: names a map or replay to open at startup.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-file` with this function. It stores the
/// value as the global data's initial file and passes it through
/// ConvertShortMapPathToLongMapPath, which in BFME 2 has a second output: a
/// label in another global data field.
///
/// GameEngine::init reads the initial file near the end of startup:
/// - A name ending in `.map` turns the shell map and the intro movie off and
///   becomes the pending file. Unless another global data flag (whose
///   meaning is not known) is set, the engine then queues `MSG_NEW_GAME`,
///   the message that LANAPI::OnGameStart, GameSpyStagingRoom::launchGame and
///   RecorderClass::playbackFile also send to start a game. A map that
///   MapCache::findMap reports as a multiplayer map is set up as a skirmish
///   with one local player slot and a seed taken from the clock.
/// - A name ending in `.rep` is played back with RecorderClass::playbackFile.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// ConvertShortMapPathToLongMapPath leaves the name alone unless it ends in
/// `.map` and contains a slash or backslash. It then expands a short name
/// such as `Maps\Name.map` to `Maps\Name\Name.map`, adding a folder named
/// after the map, and sets the label to an underscore, the map name and
/// the local date and time. What reads the label is not known.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch; `args[1]` is the path.
/// \param num  Number of arguments left, the switch included.
/// \return Always 2, even when no value follows.
///
/// \note Zero Hour (debug and internal builds only) has `-file` with a
///       one-output path conversion; BFME 1's retail table has no `-file`
///       entry.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Pass the short form of a map path. A path that already names the map's
/// own folder has that folder added a second time.
///
/// \see parseResumeGame(), GameEngine::init, RecorderClass::playbackFile,
///      MapCache::findMap


/// \fn int parseResumeGame(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-resumeGame`: the same as `-file`, plus a flag asking for
///        the game to be resumed.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-resumeGame` with this function. It is
/// parseFile() with one addition: before storing the file it sets a request
/// flag in the global data. The switch is in neither Zero Hour nor BFME 1's
/// retail table, and the code that reads the flag has not been identified,
/// so what "resume" means in practice is not known.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch; `args[1]` is the path.
/// \param num  Number of arguments left, the switch included.
/// \return Always 2.
///
/// \see parseFile()


/// \file CommandLineShellMap.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The `-noshellmap` handler, parseNoShellMap().


/// \fn int parseNoShellMap(char *args[], int)
/// \brief Handles `-noshellmap`: turns off the shell map, the map shown
///        behind the menus.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-noshellmap` with this function. It clears
/// the global data's shell-map flag and sets a second flag recording that
/// the command line turned it off. The global data's INI field table in
/// BFME 2 names the two fields `ShellMapOn` and
/// `ShellMapOffByCommandArgument`.
///
/// GameEngine::init also turns the shell map off on its own in three cases:
/// when `-file` names a map, when the `_EA_RTS_HEADLESS` environment
/// variable is set, and when the map cache does not contain the shell map
/// that the global data names. The first two also turn the intro movie off.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch (not read).
/// \return Always 1.
///
/// \note Zero Hour clears only the shell-map flag. BFME 1 already sets the
///       second flag as well, and BFME 2 keeps it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// `ShellMapOn` is an INI field of the global data, so game data can switch
/// the shell map off as well. Whether a `-mod` archive's copy of that INI
/// file is read is not established: the global data is registered, and the
/// INI files the legend lists for it are read, before the dispatcher loads
/// the mod archives (see parseMod()).
///
/// \see parseFile(), GameEngine::init


/// \file CommandLineNoAudio.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The `-noaudio` handler, parseNoAudio(), and the shared mask of
///        command-line options.
///
/// Most of the other functions in this file turn on CRC options; none of
/// them is in the switch table, and they are not documented here.


/// \fn int parseNoAudio(char *args[], int)
/// \brief Handles `-noaudio`: turns off audio, music, sounds, 3D sounds,
///        speech and ambient streams.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-noaudio` with this function. It sets one
/// bit in a process-wide mask of command-line options (see parseRif()) and,
/// when the global data exists, clears six of its flags. BFME 2's INI field
/// table for the global data names them `AudioOn`, `MusicOn`, `SoundsOn`,
/// `Sounds3DOn`, `SpeechOn` and `AmbientStreamsOn`.
///
/// GameEngine::init reads the flags at the end of its data loading: for each
/// of music, sounds, 3D sounds, speech and ambient streams it switches that
/// category on in the audio manager only if both `AudioOn` and the
/// category's own flag are set, so with this switch all five are switched
/// off at the end of startup. Other readers of the flags have not been
/// traced.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch (not read).
/// \return Always 1.
///
/// \note BFME 1's handler is the same, mask bit included. Zero Hour has
///       `-noaudio` only in debug and internal builds, and its handler
///       clears four flags: audio, speech, sounds and music.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The six flags are INI fields of the global data, so game data can turn
/// the same categories off without the switch. The switch also changes the
/// command-line checksum that online and LAN joins use (see parseRif()), so
/// a player started with `-noaudio` may be refused when joining a game whose
/// host was started without it (not tested).
///
/// \see parseRif(), GameEngine::init


/// \file CommandLineWindow.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The `-win` handler, parseWin().


/// \fn int parseWin(char *args[], int)
/// \brief Handles `-win`: sets the global data's windowed flag, so the game
///        runs in a window.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-win` with this function, which sets one
/// flag in the global data when the global data exists. Two readers are
/// known:
/// - W3DDisplay::init passes it to the display as the windowed setting
///   before it creates the render device. In a window the display sets the
///   swap interval to 0; full screen, it confines the cursor to the window.
/// - ScriptEngine::init loads the script debugger DLL only when the flag is
///   set (parseScriptDebug2()).
///
/// WinMain reads `-win` too, in its own pass over the arguments before the
/// engine starts, together with `-fullscreen`, `-xpos` and `-ypos`. Zero
/// Hour's switch table also has `-fullscreen`; BFME 2's has no such entry,
/// so only WinMain reads that switch.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch (not read).
/// \return Always 1.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The global data's INI field `Windowed` names the same flag in BFME 2's
/// field table.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Window mode is decided in two places: WinMain, before the engine exists,
/// and the global data, which the display reads. A replacement display layer
/// should take it from one source and keep the other in step.
///
/// \see W3DDisplay::init, ScriptEngine::init, parseXRes()


/// \file CommandLineResolution.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The `-xres` and `-yres` handlers, parseXRes() and parseYRes().


/// \fn int parseXRes(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-xres`: sets the screen width in pixels.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-xres` with this function. It converts the
/// value with `atoi` and stores it as the global data's horizontal
/// resolution, without a range check.
///
/// W3DDisplay::init takes the width and height from the global data, with a
/// fixed colour depth of 32 bits, and asks WW3D for a render device of that
/// size. If that fails it retries at 800 by 600 and writes those values back
/// to the global data; if the retry fails too, startup stops with a Direct3D
/// error.
///
/// The value given here is not always the one the display uses. Other code
/// writes the same field:
/// - GameLODManager::findStaticLODLevel, which W3DDisplay::init calls just
///   before it reads the size, when no static LOD level is set yet. If no
///   ideal detail level is known either, it picks the LOD preset that fits
///   the CPU, video chip and memory (800 by 600 when none does), writes that
///   preset's resolution into the field and saves it to the Options
///   preferences as `Resolution`.
/// - GlobalData::applyOptionPreferences copies the saved Options resolution
///   into the field. When it runs relative to the command line has not
///   been established.
/// - The game client's display-mode change, run from GameClient::update,
///   writes it after a successful mode switch, so it holds the current
///   width, not only the starting one. The options screen's save code
///   (AptOptions::Save) compares the selected display mode with it to decide
///   whether the mode has to change.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch; `args[1]` is the width.
/// \param num  Number of arguments left, the switch included.
/// \return 2 when a value was stored; 1 when there is no value or no global
///         data.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's parseXRes is the same, return values included.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The global data's INI fields `XResolution` and `YResolution` name the
/// same two fields in BFME 2's field table, so game data can set the
/// starting size as well.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The switch only requests a size, and the handler itself does no range
/// check. When W3DDisplay::init finds no static LOD level and no ideal
/// detail level is known yet, the detected LOD preset replaces the value,
/// and the saved Options resolution can too. The 800 by 600 fallback and
/// the 32-bit depth are fixed in W3DDisplay::init. Whether the menus and the
/// in-game interface scale to other sizes is decided elsewhere.
///
/// \see parseYRes(), W3DDisplay::init, GameLODManager::findStaticLODLevel,
///      GlobalData::applyOptionPreferences, GameClient::update,
///      AptOptions::Save


/// \fn int parseYRes(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-yres`: sets the screen height in pixels.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-yres` with this function. It is parseXRes()
/// for the vertical resolution, the field that follows the width in the
/// global data, and behaves the same way.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch; `args[1]` is the height.
/// \param num  Number of arguments left, the switch included.
/// \return 2 when a value was stored; 1 when there is no value or no global
///         data.
///
/// \see parseXRes()


/// \file CommandLineRandomSeed.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The `-randomSeed` handler, parseRandomSeed().


/// \fn int parseRandomSeed(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-randomSeed`: sets the global data's fixed random seed,
///        which then overrides the seed that games and replays start with.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-randomSeed` with this function. It
/// stores the value, converted with `atoi`, as the fixed seed; without a
/// value it stores 0. Unlike most handlers it does not check that the
/// global data exists.
///
/// The field is read by InitRandom(UnsignedInt) and InitGameLogicRandom.
/// Whenever the fixed seed is not -1, both ignore the seed they are given
/// and use the fixed seed instead. InitRandom(UnsignedInt) seeds the audio,
/// client and logic generators; InitGameLogicRandom only the logic one.
/// Their callers include LANAPI::OnGameStart and
/// GameSpyStagingRoom::launchGame, which pass the game's shared seed, the
/// skirmish start code, and RecorderClass::playbackFile, which passes the
/// seed stored in the replay. The retail global data constructor sets the
/// field to -1 (read from its retail bytes), so there is no override unless
/// this switch is given.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch; `args[1]` is the seed.
/// \param num  Number of arguments left, the switch included.
/// \return Always 1, even after reading a value.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's handler is called `parseSeed` and serves `-seed` (debug
///       and internal builds only). It checks that the global data exists,
///       leaves the field alone without a value and returns 2. BFME 2 skips
///       the check, writes 0 when there is no value and returns 1. Because
///       BFME 2's dispatcher advances by the return value (see the group
///       description), the number after `-randomSeed` is then looked up as a
///       switch itself; no switch name is a number, so it is skipped.
/// \note Zero Hour's InitRandom and InitGameLogicRandom have no fixed-seed
///       override, and the menu code that once seeded the logic generator
///       from the field is commented out. Zero Hour's GameData.ini field
///       `FixedSeed` sets the same field as `-seed`. BFME 2's INI field table
///       also has a `FixedSeed` entry, but it names a different field of the
///       global data, not the one this handler writes; what reads that field
///       is not known.
///
/// \par Modding
/// `-randomSeed` with no number fixes the seed at 0; it does not turn the
/// override off. Whether a fixed seed alone makes a skirmish game repeat
/// exactly has not been tested. In a LAN or online game the switch replaces
/// the seed all players share, so a player started with the switch would
/// seed the logic generator differently from players without it, and the
/// same applies to a replay played back with the switch. The effect on a
/// real match has not been tested.
///
/// \see InitRandom(UnsignedInt), InitGameLogicRandom, LANAPI::OnGameStart,
///      GameSpyStagingRoom::launchGame, RecorderClass::playbackFile


/// \file CommandLineFullVersion.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The `-fullVersion` handler, parseFullVersion().


/// \fn int parseFullVersion(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-fullVersion`: sets or clears `TheVersion`'s
///        show-full-version flag.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-fullVersion` with this function. It
/// converts the value with `atoi` and sets `TheVersion`'s show-full-version
/// flag when the value is not zero, or clears it when it is.
/// Version::initializeBuildMetadata clears the flag, so it is off unless
/// this switch turns it on.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch; `args[1]` is a number,
///             and any value but 0 sets the flag.
/// \param num  Number of arguments left, the switch included.
/// \return 2 when a value was read; 1 when there is no value or no
///         `TheVersion`.
///
/// \note Zero Hour returns 1 in every case. In Zero Hour the options menu's
///       version label adds the full version and build time when the flag
///       is set; the BFME 2 code that reads it has not been traced.
///
/// \see Version::initializeBuildMetadata


/// \file CommandLineScriptDebug.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The script debugger handlers, parseScriptDebug2() and
///        parseScriptDebugLite(), and the two flags they set.


/// \fn int parseScriptDebug2(char *args[], int)
/// \brief Handles `-scriptDebug2`: asks for the script debugger window.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-scriptDebug2` with this function. It sets
/// the global data's script-debug flag and a second flag (Zero Hour:
/// `m_winCursors`), and a process-wide flag that selects the Lite debugger
/// DLL. Nothing happens if the global data does not exist.
///
/// ScriptEngine::init reads these flags. When the game runs windowed and
/// script debugging is on, it loads `DebugWindowLite.dll` if the Lite flag
/// is set and `DebugWindow.dll` otherwise, then calls the DLL's
/// `CreateDebugDialog` export. If the export is missing the DLL is unloaded
/// again. Because this handler always sets the Lite flag, `-scriptDebug2`
/// loads `DebugWindowLite.dll`.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch (not read).
/// \return Always 1.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's `-scriptDebug` (`parseScriptDebug`) sets only the two
///       global data flags, so it loads `DebugWindow.dll`. BFME 1 and BFME 2
///       have the two switches documented here instead.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The debugger is a separate DLL loaded by name from the game's search
/// path, and only when the global data's windowed flag is set (by `-win` or
/// by the `Windowed` INI field, see parseWin()). If the DLL is missing
/// nothing happens. Reproducing this handler says nothing about whether a
/// debugger DLL exists or works with BFME 2.
///
/// \see parseScriptDebugLite(), parseWin(), ScriptEngine::init,
///      ScriptEngine::AppendDebugMessage


/// \fn int parseScriptDebugLite(char *args[], int)
/// \brief Handles `-scriptDebugLite`: as `-scriptDebug2`, with the debugger's
///        message log switched off.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-scriptDebugLite` with this function. It
/// sets everything parseScriptDebug2() sets, plus a second process-wide flag.
/// ScriptEngine::AppendDebugMessage returns at once while that flag is set,
/// so it sends no messages to the debugger window, and the helper that
/// reports script variable changes to the window (`_adjustVariable`)
/// returns early as well.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch (not read).
/// \return Always 1.
///
/// \see parseScriptDebug2(), ScriptEngine::AppendDebugMessage


/// \file CommandLineWatchdog.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The watchdog handlers, parseWatchdog() and parseNoWatchdog(), and the
///        flag they share.


/// \fn int parseWatchdog(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-Watchdog`: asks for the watchdog thread to be started.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-Watchdog` with this function, which sets
/// a process-wide flag. Near the end of GameEngine::init, the engine creates
/// a watchdog with createWatchdog only when the flag is set, and starts it
/// with Watchdog::start. The flag is clear by default, so the watchdog runs
/// only with this switch.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch (not read).
/// \param num  Number of arguments left (not read).
/// \return Always 1.
///
/// \note Zero Hour has neither watchdog switch; BFME 1 has both.
///
/// \see parseNoWatchdog(), createWatchdog, Watchdog::start, GameEngine::init


/// \fn int parseNoWatchdog(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-noWatchdog`: clears the flag that parseWatchdog() sets.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-noWatchdog` with this function. Since the
/// flag is clear by default, the switch matters only to undo an earlier
/// `-Watchdog` on the same command line.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch (not read).
/// \param num  Number of arguments left (not read).
/// \return Always 1.
///
/// \see parseWatchdog()


/// \file CommandLineRif.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief The `-rif` handler, parseRif().


/// \fn int parseRif(char *args[], int num)
/// \brief Handles `-rif`: sets a global data request flag whose meaning is
///        not known.
///
/// The retail switch table pairs `-rif` with this function. It sets one flag
/// in the global data, when the global data exists, and sets one bit in a
/// process-wide mask of command-line options. parseNoAudio() sets another
/// bit of the same mask, and so do several functions outside the switch
/// table. BFME 1 has the same mask; Zero Hour has none.
///
/// After the switch loop, parseCommandLine computes a checksum over the
/// mask, together with the bytes that follow it, and stores it in the
/// global data. The online custom-match join compares that checksum with
/// the room's value and refuses the join with the `GUI:JoinFailedCRCMismatch`
/// message when they differ, as it does for the INI and executable
/// checksums. A LAN join request (LANAPI::RequestGameJoin) carries it.
/// This step was read from the retail bytes of parseCommandLine.
///
/// Two of GameEngine's virtual queries answer false when the global data
/// exists with the flag clear, and otherwise ask the subsystem list (one of
/// them also asks the game logic). Several subsystems show in-game notices
/// of the form "RIF: ... reloaded" when their data is reloaded, among them
/// the armor, weapon and upgrade data; the audio events, the experience
/// levels and the string file have such notices too, in their own source
/// files. The reconstruction's `ReloadIniFileNotices.cpp`, which collects
/// several of these notices, reads RIF as "reload INI file"; this is an
/// inference from the messages. Whether `-rif` is connected to those
/// reloads is not established.
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch (not read).
/// \param num  Number of arguments left (not read).
/// \return Always 1.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Because of the mask checksum, a player started with `-rif` presents a
/// different command-line checksum from one started without it, and may be
/// refused with the CRC-mismatch message when joining (not tested; see the
/// group description).
///
/// \see parseNoAudio(), LANAPI::RequestGameJoin


/// \file CommandLine.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_commandline
/// \brief What remains here of Zero Hour's CommandLine.cpp: parseFPUPreserve().
///
/// The other handlers have files of their own. This file still declares a
/// few of them, so its page lists those as well.


/// \fn Int parseFPUPreserve(char *args[], int argc)
/// \brief Sets whether Direct3D preserves the FPU control word when the
///        device is created; no switch reaches it in BFME 2.
///
/// The value, converted with `atoi`, goes into the renderer's
/// `DX8Wrapper_PreserveFPU` setting. DX8Wrapper::Create_Device adds
/// Direct3D's FPU-preserve creation flag when the setting is not zero.
/// Without that flag, Direct3D switches the floating-point unit of the
/// thread that creates the device to single precision (Direct3D API
/// behaviour, not something this code shows).
///
/// \param args Arguments starting at the switch; `args[1]` is the setting.
/// \param argc Number of arguments left, the switch included.
/// \return Always 2, even when no value follows.
///
/// \warning No switch reaches this function in BFME 2. The retail switch
///          table does not contain it, and no pointer to it or call of it
///          has been found in the retail executable, so it appears to be
///          unused code left in the build. The name and the switch come
///          from Zero Hour, where the table pairs `-FPUPreserve` with it in
///          debug and internal builds only. The BFME 2 body was found by
///          compiling Open-BFME-1's copy of Zero Hour's CommandLine.cpp and
///          searching the retail code for the result (one hit). BFME 1's
///          retail table has no such switch either.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The engine sets the x87 unit to 24-bit precision, round to nearest,
/// itself (setFPMode) at several points, among them GameLogic::init,
/// GameLogic::getCRC and INI::loadFile. Direct3D without its FPU-preserve
/// flag also forces single precision on the creating thread (Direct3D API
/// behaviour). No other code that writes `DX8Wrapper_PreserveFPU` has been
/// found, so retail presumably creates the device without the flag. A port
/// off x87 or to another renderer must reproduce setFPMode's mode for logic
/// results to match retail; this has not been measured.
///
/// \see DX8Wrapper::Create_Device, setFPMode
